00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:01.627
they did not have that and they said,

00:22:01.627 --> 00:22:05.037
"We will give you a minivan and free gas."

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:08.280
And that was pretty much the only option

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:09.990
I was presented with,
so I was like, "Okay."

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:13.500
And I was just reminded, you
drove a minivan for many years.

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:15.240
It's probably more capacity than an SUV.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:17.910
And I'm just telling you,
man, what an invention.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:18.996
What an invention.

00:22:18.996 --> 00:22:21.090
And it pains me that-

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:22.830
It's like a tall topped station wagon.

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:25.470
I've got too much pride to
drive one on a regular basis.

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:27.180
And at this point I don't need
one, I've only got one kid

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:29.220
at home now, I don't need one.

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:31.800
There was a moment in my life
when I could have used one,

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:33.960
but I just couldn't do it.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.840
And Jessie, even more so than me,

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:39.480
she would not do the minivan,
she would not embrace

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:40.740
the minivan mom life.

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:42.030
She just was like, "I'm
not going to do it.

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:44.010
I'm not going to let that

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:46.170
grip get hold of me."

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:47.040
But we should have.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:50.790
It was a fabulous era
of time that has passed.

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:54.000
Ironically, I think that Locke and Lily,

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.310
they're both candidates for a minivan.

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:59.340
I think that it could
draw some trouble, but

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:00.340
you remember Greg

00:23:02.149 --> 00:23:03.843
had a minivan in college.

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:07.596
Having a minivan in college is cool.

00:23:07.596 --> 00:23:10.020
That was an ironic,

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:12.783
totally functional party choice.

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:16.260
Low to the ground,

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:17.460
drives like a car,

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:19.710
capacity of a truck.

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:20.543
You can walk right into it.

00:23:20.543 --> 00:23:22.203
It's like a walk-in shower.

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:29.163
You got USB-C, you got USB
whatever the other one is,

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:31.650
you can plug anything in.

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:33.360
Just very impressed with minivans.

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:34.680
Yeah.

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:37.110
You can be an adult
sitting in the middle seat

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:39.190
back there and you it's like you're at

00:23:40.380 --> 00:23:42.030
a Star Trek console.

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:42.900
You're sitting up right.

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:44.010
Feel very, very-
You're ready for anything.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:44.843
Advanced.

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:47.280
And very aerodynamic.

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:49.830
Now, we got a bunch of stuff,

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:53.973
and by the time we had packed
the car to its fullness,

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:57.720
it was Tetris.

00:23:57.720 --> 00:24:00.510
In fact, Jessie took a picture,

00:24:00.510 --> 00:24:01.860
we're going to show it right now,

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:04.860
cause she took a picture
when I had created the first

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.200
base layer of the Tetris
in the back of the van.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:09.367
She's like, "Send that to Link."

00:24:09.367 --> 00:24:11.970
I would loved it.

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:14.370
Most of the times in my
life when I'm packing things

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:16.950
that you got to get a bunch
of stuff, I'm with you

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:19.200
and you are so committed to it that I just

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:20.950
hand you things and you just do it.

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:24.597
But this was a moment where I was like

00:24:24.597 --> 00:24:26.220
there be no could be no wasted space here,

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:28.507
so I'm saying things like,

00:24:28.507 --> 00:24:29.760
"Turn that 90 degrees."

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:31.890
I'm actually just standing
there and Locke is doing

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:32.936
the stuff and I'm saying-

00:24:32.936 --> 00:24:34.680
Oh wow, that's the life.

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:37.263
And he's like, "Dad, this is crazy."

00:24:37.263 --> 00:24:39.120
And I was like, "Hey, I
guess I could have done this

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:40.290
all along."

00:24:40.290 --> 00:24:41.910
I mean, every once in a
while I got to do Tetris.

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:42.870
No, Rhett.

00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:45.420
Again, you learned from the best.

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:46.680
I've never watched you.

00:24:46.680 --> 00:24:47.610
You've watched me.

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:49.860
No, no. I just hand you
the packages and move away.

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:50.693
You felt it.

00:24:50.693 --> 00:24:52.800
I've never ever actually
watched you closely.

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.630
Yes you have, man.

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:55.860
That's how you know.

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:58.980
But we packed the whole,
obviously you've got the

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:00.570
first row and the second
row and then third row

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:02.970
is folded down and then
everything, ceiling to floor,

00:25:02.970 --> 00:25:05.250
all the way back based
on stuff we had brought,

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:08.700
plus stuff we had bought,
was all the way full.

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:10.710
To the point where when you closed it,

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:13.830
you were catching stuff to
make sure it doesn't fall off.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:15.450
Yeah. Good work.

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:16.283
Now

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:22.200
the process of moving in to
college where Locke's going,

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:25.470
and I don't know if this was
the case where Lily went,

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:29.730
but it also was the fact that
his dorm room that he is in,

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:31.500
he's on the 12th floor.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:35.130
Good gosh.
Of a very large building.

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:36.330
Okay.

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:39.540
And what they do is,

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:43.170
you check in and then
you get these little tags

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:46.230
and then you get into a line
and you have a specified window

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:47.063
of time.

00:25:47.063 --> 00:25:47.896
So it was like-

00:25:47.896 --> 00:25:49.320
So everybody's not moving at once.

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:53.193
You have to be there at
5:00 PM was our window.

00:25:54.270 --> 00:25:58.440
And you drive under this set
of awnings that they've set up,

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:00.780
because it's Florida and it might rain,

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.990
and you park and you open your thing

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:05.310
and then all these people.

00:26:05.310 --> 00:26:06.810
Oh wow.
Not you.

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:10.530
A bunch of people in certain shirts,

00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:13.410
take all your stuff and
put it in these giant bins

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:16.200
and then you just drive and park,

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:18.300
and then they take it, they go in,

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:21.540
they use the elevators
exclusively, so no parents or kids

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:23.730
are using the elevators,
get your stuff in your room

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:26.400
so when you get to your room
it's either they're in there

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:27.870
or they're bringing all the bins in there,

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:29.194
so you don't have to lift anything heavy.

00:26:29.194 --> 00:26:30.055
What?

00:26:30.055 --> 00:26:31.677
Did you have to walk up
12 flights of stairs.

00:26:31.677 --> 00:26:33.750
And they make everybody
walk, so we had to walk up

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:34.650
12 flights of stairs.

00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:35.640
Oh, seriously?

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:37.770
Yeah, but we didn't have anything.

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:39.120
We had given it to everyone.

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:40.170
That's the system.

00:26:40.170 --> 00:26:41.850
Well, how many flights are there?

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:42.683
How much higher could you go?

00:26:42.683 --> 00:26:43.650
There's just another couple of floors.

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:44.760
14 or 15 or something like that.

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:46.197
Oh man.

00:26:46.197 --> 00:26:48.510
Didn't get the penthouse.
That's a long ways.

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:50.910
I guess that's a decent
trade for not having

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:52.770
to shuttle anything up.

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:53.603
Yeah.

00:26:55.564 --> 00:26:58.680
Or course I'm totally dading
out about this, cause I'm like,

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:00.390
how long have they had
this system in place?

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:01.263
Did they start?

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:03.540
How many years have they done this system?

00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:06.270
It's quite inefficient
system. I'm very impressed.

00:27:06.270 --> 00:27:09.960
But this was the moment, as we
were pulling up in the line,

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:14.700
when I began to really feel
the difference that Jessie

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:17.100
and I bring to this particular event.

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:19.020
Now I don't know if you
remember what it was like

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:21.750
for us to move into college,
but as I was trying to remember

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:23.700
and tell Locke and Jessie and Shepherd;

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:29.430
I was like, "I don't think my
parents went up to NC State

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:30.263
with us.

00:27:30.263 --> 00:27:31.096
No, no, no.

00:27:31.096 --> 00:27:32.610
I'm almost positive they didn't.

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:33.443
It was

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:38.250
my papa took his truck,

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:39.780
we put everything in
the back of his truck.

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:41.430
I knew you would remember this.

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:47.640
Yeah, so he drove so he could
drive his truck back home.

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.160
And so he's the one who helped move us in.

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:50.993
And he didn't-

00:27:50.993 --> 00:27:51.930
And there were no stairs.

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:54.330
And he didn't have any opinions about.

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:55.650
No.

00:27:55.650 --> 00:27:58.170
The whole point of us
going into our dorm room

00:27:58.170 --> 00:28:01.470
was to get us and our stuff into the room

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:04.410
and then it's up to you
guys to make it look

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:07.500
the way you want to, organize
your stuff, hang stuff up.

00:28:07.500 --> 00:28:11.040
Of course generational
differences, first of all,

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.980
but also just family dynamics.

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:17.640
My parents were super
loving and supportive,

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:21.480
but they weren't involved
in those kinds of details.

00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:24.660
But back in the day, when
Jessie was going off to college,

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:28.440
her mom was totally involved
in making her dorm room

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:30.510
look like a section of Wet Seal.

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:31.350
You remember that store?
Oh god.

00:28:31.350 --> 00:28:33.990
You know what I mean?
Clothes hanging everywhere?

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:35.940
No.
Like faux leather pants

00:28:35.940 --> 00:28:37.470
and big fluffy shirts?

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:40.173
I just mean intention,

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:43.290
well, that was what Jessie
was wearing at the time.

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:45.840
But yeah, they put up purple wallpaper

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:49.230
on one whole wall inside the dorm room.

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:50.700
Yeah, that's not allowed.

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:52.770
They got away with it because
their room had not been

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:55.650
painted along with other
rooms or something.

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:56.483
Point being-

00:28:56.483 --> 00:28:59.190
Did Locke just know I'm going
to let her do her thing?

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:01.530
Well, in fact,

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:03.840
I was just looking at Jessie's
Instagram post about him

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:06.030
kind of going to the college,
and she was saying that

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:07.110
he's been super sweet.

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:10.170
And actually he was like,
"Yeah, I want my mom,

00:29:10.170 --> 00:29:11.430
my mom is good at this.

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:13.830
If she's going to do the work
and she's going to be invested

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:15.180
in this."

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:19.320
But also Jessie gave Locke the quiz

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:22.650
that she gives to
prospective design clients

00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:25.830
to find out what your style is

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:27.810
cause this is his style.

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:29.130
I didn't even know that this was happening

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:30.837
until I look at my wife's Instagram.

00:29:30.837 --> 00:29:34.530
But it helps explain why
we had so much in that van.

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:35.363
Yeah.

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:38.300
And I'm sitting there-
Wonder what his roommate

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:39.993
thought of all this?

00:29:39.993 --> 00:29:41.880
They thought it was great.

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:43.110
His roommate thought it was great.

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:44.479
And also-
That's what he told you.

00:29:44.479 --> 00:29:47.190
No, and also, first of all,

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:50.190
this isn't something exclusive to Jessie.

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:53.760
You could kind of see parents
are just way more involved

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.940
in kids lives than they
were when we were coming up

00:29:56.940 --> 00:29:59.303
about stuff like this and
their mom and dad are in there,

00:29:59.303 --> 00:30:01.003
they're putting stuff on the wall.

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:04.020
So this is not just
something that's happening,

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:06.720
but it's on one end of the
spectrum when it comes to Jessie,

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:09.443
and I'm on the other end of
the spectrum, cause I'm like,

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:14.400
you're thinking about
the colors of things?

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:17.610
Just put him in the room
and hug him and say bye.

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:18.443
Right. Yeah.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:23.280
But the way that this
translates into your minivan

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:26.550
is it's full of stuff,
and so as we're pulling up

00:30:26.550 --> 00:30:27.950
to this thing, I'm thinking,

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:30.750
I'm looking at the other families.

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:32.460
There's a family in a hatchback

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:34.350
and the family gets out of
the hatchback and then just

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:37.300
opens the hatchback and that's
all the stuff this girl has.

00:30:38.212 --> 00:30:39.045
And then I'm like, oh,
there's a family with-

00:30:39.045 --> 00:30:40.800
She has to build a new life on our own.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:42.570
There's a family with a pickup truck

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:45.007
and the bed of the truck,

00:30:45.007 --> 00:30:47.400
it's an extend cab, so
the better of the truck's

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:50.190
not too long and the
stuff's not popping up

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:52.145
over the end of the thing.

00:30:52.145 --> 00:30:56.403
But we have Tetrised an entire minivan.

00:30:57.240 --> 00:31:00.900
And when we open the door,
things are going to fall out.

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:02.670
I'm beginning to feel shame.

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:03.780
Yeah.

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:05.250
And then I'm thinking,
well, how many boxes

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:06.210
are they going to give us?

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:08.550
How many of these big ass
crates are we going to fill up?

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:10.100
And who's going to be watching?

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:12.600
I know this is all irrational.

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:14.412
And you wish you had a disguise again.

00:31:14.412 --> 00:31:15.270
Oh yeah.

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:17.280
And I'd already been recognized by a few,

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:19.290
as we keep sneaking through this thing,

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:22.237
we've got our windows rolled
down and there's families.

00:31:22.237 --> 00:31:23.220
"Rhett!

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:25.327
Good mythical morning!"
and stuff like that.

00:31:25.327 --> 00:31:28.997
"Where's your buddy?"

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:34.450
So we get to there and we open it up and

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:38.910
it was almost like the
guys with the big bins

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:42.120
had been prepped to
not let you feel shame.

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:42.953
Okay.

00:31:42.953 --> 00:31:43.786
Because

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:46.957
we opened the thing and
then Jessie was like,

00:31:46.957 --> 00:31:48.420
"My husband feels shame."

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:51.510
She said it, "My husband feels
shame about how much stuff

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:52.343
we had."

00:31:52.343 --> 00:31:54.210
And he was like, "Oh no,
we've seen more than this."

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:57.682
And then he was like, "How many carts?

00:31:57.682 --> 00:32:00.663
What's the most number of
carts that we filled up today?"

00:32:00.663 --> 00:32:02.970
as they were filling up the fourth cart,

00:32:02.970 --> 00:32:04.500
he said, "I think it's five or six."

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:07.230
And then another guy was
being honest, said four.

00:32:07.230 --> 00:32:10.410
So I was like, okay, so
we're tied for the record.

00:32:10.410 --> 00:32:11.760
Four of these big ass bins.

00:32:12.960 --> 00:32:15.450
But just kind of going through that

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:17.523
relieved a little tension.

00:32:17.523 --> 00:32:21.453
Getting up to the room and
then beginning the process;

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:28.383
she wanted to do a headboard.

00:32:30.360 --> 00:32:33.510
A headboard that you stick to the wall.

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:35.969
I love the fact that none of
this is about Locke at all.

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:40.313
We'll get to that.

00:32:40.313 --> 00:32:42.123
A sticky headboard.

00:32:43.034 --> 00:32:44.610
A what?

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:46.230
Isn't it a bunk bed?

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:47.063
No, it's not.

00:32:47.063 --> 00:32:50.460
Beds are on the ground on
each side of the thing.

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:54.000
And there's no option for
making them in the bunk beds

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:55.200
in this particular room.

00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:58.350
It'd be helpful if there were
because it would be bigger.

00:32:58.350 --> 00:32:59.550
Cause it's small.

00:32:59.550 --> 00:33:01.530
Smaller than our dorm room.
What?

00:33:01.530 --> 00:33:02.680
But I did tell Locke,

00:33:03.516 --> 00:33:05.790
"Hey, this is the same
size the one I was in

00:33:05.790 --> 00:33:06.690
my freshman year."

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:11.580
But the headboard, I'm going
to point out the headboard

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:13.960
because it's kind of indicative of

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:17.310
the difference in approach.

00:33:17.310 --> 00:33:18.393
First of all, Locke,

00:33:21.420 --> 00:33:23.670
he's empowered Jessie.

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:25.860
Didn't it to have a headboard?

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:26.693
No.

00:33:27.720 --> 00:33:28.893
It's just a bed-

00:33:29.820 --> 00:33:31.920
With a mattress.

00:33:31.920 --> 00:33:33.213
It's a frame basically.

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:35.580
There is no headboard.
But then there's a wall

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:36.657
that it's up against that,

00:33:36.657 --> 00:33:39.870
and this is a common thing,
you can put the headboard

00:33:39.870 --> 00:33:42.570
up on the wall, you can mount
it on the wall above a bed.

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:44.670
I understand.
And this is made for

00:33:44.670 --> 00:33:47.760
a twin bed, which is what
you get in a dorm room.

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:51.540
But there's a weird thing with
the way that there's a board

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:54.220
and then it's got these
sticky things on it,

00:33:54.220 --> 00:33:56.790
cause you can't put any holes in the wall,

00:33:56.790 --> 00:33:58.680
so you have to do it
with these sticky things,

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:00.600
but they weren't lined up right.

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:03.990
I spent a lot of time on this headboard,

00:34:03.990 --> 00:34:04.823
and then

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:07.950
eventually had to abandon
the headboard because

00:34:07.950 --> 00:34:09.450
it just wouldn't work.

00:34:09.450 --> 00:34:12.450
And when I get into my
emotional processing,

00:34:12.450 --> 00:34:14.050
I'll come back to the headboard.

00:34:16.260 --> 00:34:17.403
But I think that,

00:34:18.660 --> 00:34:22.480
first of all, I witnessed so many families

00:34:24.570 --> 00:34:27.480
impatiently being short with each other

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:29.160
or outright yelling at each other

00:34:29.160 --> 00:34:31.170
as we would go around
to the different stores.

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:32.460
Tensions are high.

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:35.790
We went to the Target and
everyone's buying stuff.

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:38.430
And we didn't even go
to the closest Target,

00:34:38.430 --> 00:34:40.860
we went to the second closest Target,

00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:42.980
and still, they had all descended upon it.

00:34:42.980 --> 00:34:43.813
Oh yeah.

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:46.620
People just mad at each other.

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:50.940
Just family's falling apart
at this integral time.

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:52.800
And I was like, we're just
another one of these families

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:54.093
that's mad at each other.

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:57.660
We weren't really being
mean to each other,

00:34:57.660 --> 00:35:00.870
but I was definitely
frustrated cause I was like,

00:35:00.870 --> 00:35:05.130
I'm trying to deal with this emotionally

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:07.230
and trying to figure out
what is it that I'm feeling,

00:35:07.230 --> 00:35:09.360
which is always a difficult thing for me.

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:12.120
And we're trying to make
his room feel this way

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:14.373
and it doesn't feel like a priority to me.

00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:18.090
And this dad is yelling at his
son because the son just said

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:19.503
something mean to the mom.

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:23.100
And I just kept witnessing
these situations.

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:25.080
But it also made me feel
good cause I was like-

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:26.220
Makes it easier to part ways.

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:29.010
We're just another family.

00:35:29.010 --> 00:35:31.380
We're just another family
that's dealing with this

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:32.760
momentous thing.

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:34.650
Yeah. At least you're not in Florida.

00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:35.703
Oh, sorry.

00:35:38.670 --> 00:35:40.390
So to talk a little bit about

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:42.963
the processing of this.

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:46.713
From Locke's perspective,

00:35:52.537 --> 00:35:53.370
he's been simultaneously
super excited about school,

00:35:57.510 --> 00:36:00.720
but also increasingly anxious about like,

00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:03.420
oh, I'm not going to be at home anymore,

00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:05.850
and I'm also not going
to be in the same city

00:36:05.850 --> 00:36:06.993
or the same state.

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:10.203
Or the same time zone or coast.

00:36:11.490 --> 00:36:13.680
That's beginning to sink in with him.

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:16.770
So a lot of his conversation
was around just,

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:18.810
and of course Locke says
exactly what he's thinking,

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:21.000
exactly what he's feeling,

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.790
which is a huge difference between

00:36:23.790 --> 00:36:25.030
me at his age

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:28.590
and him.

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:32.433
So we are constantly discussing
and talking about things.

00:36:34.710 --> 00:36:38.490
There's some anxiety around
just the transition for him.

00:36:38.490 --> 00:36:39.870
I mean he handled it really, really well

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:41.880
and he's doing great and I've
talked to him a couple times

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:44.670
since I've gotten back
and he's really adjusting,

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:45.730
meeting people and

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:50.010
beginning to embrace the I am on my own,

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:51.840
but also not really.

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:53.820
They're not really on their own.

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:57.150
They can FaceTime with us at any moment.

00:36:57.150 --> 00:36:59.490
And obviously if there's
something that they need,

00:36:59.490 --> 00:37:00.813
it can be arranged.

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:06.063
It's not as on their own as
they will feel in four years.

00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:15.090
One of the things I've
been thinking about,

00:37:15.090 --> 00:37:17.130
first of all, there's a lot
of stuff going on right now

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:18.330
in life.

00:37:18.330 --> 00:37:21.780
There's just a number of
things, I'm not going to go

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:25.830
into detail and nothing in
particular is standing out,

00:37:25.830 --> 00:37:28.290
but this is a really busy time of year,

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:31.800
there's things that are
stressful and anxiety inducing,

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:34.350
and then there's this
thing that's happening

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:36.570
where your son is going off the college

00:37:36.570 --> 00:37:40.173
and much like you kind of
communicated last time,

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:46.173
you're trying to make it into something.

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:50.220
Dads tend to do this
too, make it meaningful,

00:37:50.220 --> 00:37:52.710
and you want to say the right
thing at the right time,

00:37:52.710 --> 00:37:54.747
but you also want to feel;

00:37:54.747 --> 00:37:56.970
and this isn't something
that I would've done 10,

00:37:56.970 --> 00:38:00.060
15 years ago, but I want to
make sure that I'm feeling this

00:38:00.060 --> 00:38:02.250
in the way that I should
feel it and processing it

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:04.050
in the way that I should process it,

00:38:04.050 --> 00:38:06.360
which is more of a result of therapy

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:08.610
and also understanding
that you can have feelings

00:38:08.610 --> 00:38:10.800
and you can process those feelings.

00:38:10.800 --> 00:38:12.450
Which I've gotten much better at.

00:38:14.190 --> 00:38:19.190
But I've also been anticipating
the really intense,

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:24.150
emotional reaction, the walking
through the neighborhood

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:26.850
crying situation that happened with you.

00:38:26.850 --> 00:38:28.110
Yeah.

00:38:28.110 --> 00:38:29.940
And what I realized, well, first of all,

00:38:29.940 --> 00:38:31.190
that hasn't happened yet.

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:35.080
But what has happened is

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:39.063
a series of 10,

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:42.810
over the past couple of months,

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:45.960
10 moments of emotion

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:49.293
that will be some degree of crying.

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:51.930
It might just be like
welling up a little bit.

00:38:51.930 --> 00:38:53.700
It might be weeping a little bit.

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:54.533
Okay.

00:38:54.533 --> 00:38:56.170
The way I describe it is

00:38:57.699 --> 00:38:59.613
when you've shaken up a soda,

00:39:01.290 --> 00:39:04.110
the bottle, and you're like,
if I open this all at once

00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:05.558
it's going to go crazy, but if I just go.

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:08.940
Open it a little bit at a time,

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:11.010
by the time I fully open
it, it'll be just like,

00:39:11.010 --> 00:39:12.600
oh, it's just a soda.

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:15.510
And I think that that
might be what's happening.

00:39:15.510 --> 00:39:18.940
Because I've had a couple
of moments of really

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:21.420
emotional processing.

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:25.260
The first was putting
together his slideshow for his

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:26.700
graduation thing that you came to.

00:39:26.700 --> 00:39:27.660
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:39:27.660 --> 00:39:30.450
And looking at all these photos and

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:32.940
organizing them and
picking his favorite songs

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:33.773
and putting in.

00:39:34.890 --> 00:39:37.620
There was an intense moment
I was sitting in bed editing

00:39:37.620 --> 00:39:39.420
and I was crying.

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:42.330
So that was the beginning
of processing it.

00:39:42.330 --> 00:39:43.280
The second thing is

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:47.910
I wrote him and Shepherd a song.

00:39:47.910 --> 00:39:48.743
Right.

00:39:54.598 --> 00:39:56.550
It was part of the process
of thinking about your

00:39:56.550 --> 00:39:59.700
parenting changing as you deconstruct, but

00:39:59.700 --> 00:40:03.603
I was really thinking about
Locke, especially leaving.

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:06.030
Here he goes. He's going off.

00:40:06.030 --> 00:40:08.010
It's like, you've done what you can do.

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:10.200
He's going to be dealing with
that for the rest of his life.

00:40:10.200 --> 00:40:11.223
Whatever you did.

00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:15.093
And then playing him the song.

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:18.990
That was the last song that I played.

00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:20.640
I played it for him and Shepherd.

00:40:21.610 --> 00:40:23.719
You played the track,
you didn't play a guitar.

00:40:23.719 --> 00:40:25.051
Oh, I could have never played it for him,

00:40:25.051 --> 00:40:26.880
I would be a mess, I wouldn't
be able to make it through it.

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:28.230
So yes, I played the track.

00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:34.110
Locke was driving his car,
me and him and Shepherd

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:36.690
went out to eat or whatever
and then we played the song

00:40:36.690 --> 00:40:37.523
and it was

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:40.823
crying.

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:46.620
Tears were welling up in Locke as well.

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:48.840
I didn't really look back at Shepherd.

00:40:48.840 --> 00:40:50.640
It was emotional for all three of us.

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:51.720
Sure, yeah.
But there was this

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:52.750
emotional moment

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:56.883
and then we spent some
time talking after that.

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:00.380
And then there's been,

00:41:01.950 --> 00:41:05.163
tying it back into to your
story about Lily last year,

00:41:06.750 --> 00:41:09.460
I think I actually remember
saying my application

00:41:13.140 --> 00:41:17.280
is I want to make this
year count with Locke.

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:18.570
Cause Locke,

00:41:18.570 --> 00:41:20.220
he's super independent.

00:41:20.220 --> 00:41:21.930
He's always gone.

00:41:21.930 --> 00:41:23.980
He's always hanging out with his friends.

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:29.550
This summer he's traveled
with his friends.

00:41:29.550 --> 00:41:30.963
He's been gone.

00:41:32.100 --> 00:41:35.100
So it's not every night
you walk in, there's Locke.

00:41:35.100 --> 00:41:38.370
It's like, no, Locke's
probably not going to be there.

00:41:38.370 --> 00:41:42.540
So he's sort of already kind
of established his independence

00:41:42.540 --> 00:41:45.150
in his not being at home.

00:41:45.150 --> 00:41:48.750
But I was like, I want to make
the most of the time we have.

00:41:48.750 --> 00:41:52.083
And so that was my kind of goal.

00:41:53.010 --> 00:41:55.440
And then we had those two road trips.

00:41:55.440 --> 00:41:56.550
We had the road trip over Christmas,

00:41:56.550 --> 00:41:57.780
we had the road trip over spring break,

00:41:57.780 --> 00:41:59.490
which those were awesome.

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:01.890
And that was actually, as
we finished in Florida,

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:04.150
that was one of the reasons he chose

00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:06.260
the school that he chose.

00:42:08.400 --> 00:42:11.670
And then we've just had more
just hanging out as friends

00:42:11.670 --> 00:42:13.210
time where it's not

00:42:14.610 --> 00:42:16.770
this is a dad and this is a son,

00:42:16.770 --> 00:42:19.140
and the dad is saying things to the son

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:21.840
to try to correct him or guide him

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:24.000
and the son is challenging.

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:25.440
That's been a lot of our relationship

00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:26.703
cause he's a challenger.

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:32.100
But this past year has
been more of a friendship.

00:42:32.100 --> 00:42:33.720
And there's been a couple of
times where we just hung out

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:35.613
and just talked.

00:42:36.450 --> 00:42:38.550
And I have to hold
myself back a little bit

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:41.790
from going into dad
mode and advice mode or

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:44.160
here's a principle that
you should take with you

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:46.320
and just be like, let's just connect.

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:47.850
And so there's been more of that.

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:50.103
More than ever this past year.

00:42:52.050 --> 00:42:56.130
And then, when we were in Florida,

00:42:56.130 --> 00:42:59.040
we moved him in basically,
and then we hung out;

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:01.530
or no, this was the night
before we moved him in.

00:43:01.530 --> 00:43:04.710
And me and him just went out
and just walked around town

00:43:04.710 --> 00:43:07.960
for a while and just spent
a bunch of time talking

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:13.290
about everything, including
how he was feeling

00:43:13.290 --> 00:43:14.123
and everything.

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:18.630
And then in addition to that,
there's just been multiple

00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:21.930
times where I'll see something,

00:43:21.930 --> 00:43:24.360
I'll look at his room or
whatever and there'll be

00:43:24.360 --> 00:43:25.193
just a moment of

00:43:26.430 --> 00:43:28.050
emotion where

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:29.430
tears will start

00:43:29.430 --> 00:43:30.510
and I'll kind of let it happen.

00:43:30.510 --> 00:43:34.353
But again, it doesn't turn into
an avalanche or a waterfall.

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:38.010
Yeah, it's just these
moments of I'm living

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:39.990
this milestone moment.

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:40.823
Yeah.

00:43:43.365 --> 00:43:45.300
It's not just about
acknowledging emotions,

00:43:45.300 --> 00:43:48.810
but acknowledging the experience.

00:43:48.810 --> 00:43:50.523
Emotions are a part of it.

00:43:53.850 --> 00:43:55.707
It's about fully processing things,

00:43:55.707 --> 00:43:57.270
and they're a component of it.

00:43:57.270 --> 00:43:58.443
But it's not like,

00:44:01.373 --> 00:44:02.730
when am I going to break down?

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:04.480
Well, but I just
anticipated that happening.

00:44:04.480 --> 00:44:06.780
Cause I acknowledge that's what I made it

00:44:06.780 --> 00:44:07.953
out to be a lot.

00:44:09.709 --> 00:44:11.880
I forced it.

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:16.350
And then now I have a
mitigated perspective on it.

00:44:16.350 --> 00:44:19.290
Cause you keep seeing Lily.

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:20.250
Yeah.
She keeps coming home.

00:44:20.250 --> 00:44:22.400
She keeps coming home. I keep seeing her.

00:44:23.250 --> 00:44:24.780
Now that's going to be
less the case for a Locke

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:26.820
cause he is going to be further away.

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:29.970
But I think we'll be more connected

00:44:29.970 --> 00:44:34.053
via phone than we ever
are when he's in town.

00:44:37.170 --> 00:44:38.130
Cause it's the only way to do it.

00:44:38.130 --> 00:44:39.060
Yeah.

00:44:39.060 --> 00:44:43.050
Yeah, but in that moment,
in those couple of days,

00:44:43.050 --> 00:44:46.080
yeah, it's just having
those times when it's

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:47.560
an acknowledgement of

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:52.710
you're living this
trope, you're living this

00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:54.700
milestone moment that

00:44:56.936 --> 00:44:58.890
you'll never have it again.

00:44:58.890 --> 00:45:02.580
Even if he goes somewhere
else and when Shepherd

00:45:02.580 --> 00:45:05.760
goes somewhere and same with my kids.

00:45:05.760 --> 00:45:07.590
The first kid going off to college

00:45:07.590 --> 00:45:12.240
and everything associated
with it is special.

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:15.810
But the logistics of it do
get in the way sometimes.

00:45:15.810 --> 00:45:18.120
Oh, I've got a brave Target.

00:45:18.120 --> 00:45:19.380
We're upset with each other.

00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:21.450
Well, I have a very specific

00:45:21.450 --> 00:45:24.870
thing that happened at
the moment of truth.

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:26.253
My adult life,

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:30.990
especially the past 10 years in California

00:45:30.990 --> 00:45:33.303
and the past, what, five years in therapy,

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:37.893
you know the McLaughlins,

00:45:39.240 --> 00:45:41.433
we don't make a big deal out of anything.

00:45:42.330 --> 00:45:45.030
When people make a big deal
about something we're like

00:45:45.960 --> 00:45:48.390
does this person got a weakness?

00:45:48.390 --> 00:45:50.460
What is it about this person
that they're making a big deal

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:53.310
about this because we don't
make a big deal about anything.

00:45:53.310 --> 00:45:55.060
Does this person have a weakness?

00:45:57.420 --> 00:45:59.250
Yes.
I was asking Shepherd.

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:00.300
We all do.

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:03.240
Shepherd last night, I was like,

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:05.250
cause me and she are
just hanging out together

00:46:05.250 --> 00:46:06.950
as we wait for Jessie to get back.

00:46:08.031 --> 00:46:09.300
She still trying to get that headboard up?

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:11.317
Yeah, probably.

00:46:11.317 --> 00:46:13.860
I hope at least she takes a picture of it.

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:16.097
I'm asking him how he's processing it and

00:46:16.097 --> 00:46:17.700
he was like, "Do you remember

00:46:17.700 --> 00:46:19.590
when Cole went off to college?"

00:46:19.590 --> 00:46:20.423
And I was like,

00:46:22.177 --> 00:46:23.010
"No."

00:46:26.910 --> 00:46:30.780
But I would not be
surprised if we did not hug.

00:46:30.780 --> 00:46:32.880
Just to be completely honest with you.

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:34.770
Now I would say that for a couple reasons;

00:46:34.770 --> 00:46:37.020
number one, he was just
going to Chapel Hill,

00:46:37.020 --> 00:46:40.470
and so he was going to be 45 minutes away.

00:46:40.470 --> 00:46:43.743
And so it was like, oh, you're
just going to be up the road.

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:48.090
My family didn't make moments of things.

00:46:48.090 --> 00:46:50.100
Jessie's family makes moments of things.

00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:53.080
I remember the first birthday
celebration I went to

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:55.810
for somebody, I can't
remember, and I was like,

00:46:55.810 --> 00:46:56.880
whoa, you guys-

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:58.020
Somebody was having a moment.

00:46:58.020 --> 00:47:00.090
I'm like, you guys make
a moment of everything.

00:47:00.090 --> 00:47:01.650
My family, we're just kind of,

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:04.110
we're not Jehovah's witnesses,
we don't not believe

00:47:04.110 --> 00:47:05.223
in birthdays.

00:47:06.810 --> 00:47:08.147
Or blood transfusions.

00:47:08.147 --> 00:47:10.410
You're going to get your
fried chicken and your cake

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:11.790
and again, I don't want to miscommunicate,

00:47:11.790 --> 00:47:14.643
it's not that there wasn't a love there,

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:19.323
it wasn't sentimental outwardly.

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:22.800
I'm not saying I know we didn't hug,

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:24.900
but I'm just saying I
wouldn't be surprised.

00:47:24.900 --> 00:47:26.820
I know that there wasn't
some moment where it was like

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:29.160
let's hug and let's say
things to each other

00:47:29.160 --> 00:47:31.170
in the way that Locke and Shepherd.

00:47:31.170 --> 00:47:35.340
In fact, I started filming
them in the room as they

00:47:35.340 --> 00:47:36.690
hugged each other and stuff

00:47:38.190 --> 00:47:41.740
and then Locke said something
offensive to Shepherd

00:47:43.200 --> 00:47:45.540
in order to-
Break the tension.

00:47:45.540 --> 00:47:47.430
Because he was like, "Dad,
it was stupid that you were

00:47:47.430 --> 00:47:49.130
filming us, I was just trying to"-

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:53.430
Make it unusable.
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:47:53.430 --> 00:47:54.347
He just said something mean to Shepherd,

00:47:54.347 --> 00:47:56.163
but it was obviously in jest.

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:01.840
My adult life has been figuring out

00:48:03.870 --> 00:48:05.919
that isn't who I am.

00:48:05.919 --> 00:48:07.680
That's the way that we did things,

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:10.530
but A, not the person I married.

00:48:10.530 --> 00:48:12.720
I married somebody on the
opposite end of the spectrum

00:48:12.720 --> 00:48:15.270
in terms of making things into a moment

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:16.833
and expressing themselves.

00:48:25.950 --> 00:48:27.060
It isn't that I don't have feelings,

00:48:27.060 --> 00:48:29.280
I actually have a whole lot,
but they come out sideways

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:30.490
because they don't

00:48:31.590 --> 00:48:33.960
process in a healthy way all the time.

00:48:33.960 --> 00:48:35.193
They do more and more.

00:48:37.230 --> 00:48:39.840
But anyway, that was an
interesting thing to hear Shepherd

00:48:39.840 --> 00:48:41.610
talk about and ask me that question.

00:48:41.610 --> 00:48:42.630
I was like, "Man, I don't know.

00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:45.090
We just didn't do things in this way."

00:48:45.090 --> 00:48:47.130
But I'm a different person now.

00:48:47.130 --> 00:48:50.667
And so I am thinking about this moment,

00:48:50.667 --> 00:48:52.320
and the way that it worked was-

00:48:52.320 --> 00:48:53.997
Meaning the goodbye?
The goodbye.

00:48:53.997 --> 00:48:56.640
The send off.
We're in his room

00:48:56.640 --> 00:48:58.500
and this is day two.

00:48:58.500 --> 00:49:00.720
Day one was moving all
the stuff up and beginning

00:49:00.720 --> 00:49:03.270
to put it in, but because
we moved in at 5:00 PM

00:49:03.270 --> 00:49:06.210
and then he had an event
he had to go to that night,

00:49:06.210 --> 00:49:09.180
Jessie and I kind of stayed
in his room til 9:00, 9:30

00:49:09.180 --> 00:49:10.710
and then we left and then we were like,

00:49:10.710 --> 00:49:12.240
we're going to come back
tomorrow, there's a few things

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:15.120
that we need to get and then
I'll just leave from here

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:16.170
to go to the airport.

00:49:18.030 --> 00:49:21.150
And so we're in his room,

00:49:21.150 --> 00:49:23.220
and it took us a while to
get a couple of things,

00:49:23.220 --> 00:49:25.470
so we had one hour in his room

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:27.480
and I had to get the damn headboard up!

00:49:27.480 --> 00:49:30.300
Still dealing with the damn headboard!

00:49:30.300 --> 00:49:32.340
This is headboard take two.

00:49:32.340 --> 00:49:34.590
Have a new idea for what I'm going to do

00:49:34.590 --> 00:49:36.900
and it may involve violating school policy

00:49:36.900 --> 00:49:38.523
and putting a nail in the wall.

00:49:40.110 --> 00:49:42.180
It didn't cause it didn't work.

00:49:42.180 --> 00:49:44.580
But at some point I'm looking
in the clock and I'm like,

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:47.730
okay, I have 12 minutes cause
I've timed this of course,

00:49:47.730 --> 00:49:49.230
to be able to get to the airport on time,

00:49:49.230 --> 00:49:51.270
I have 12 minutes left in
the room based on the time

00:49:51.270 --> 00:49:53.070
it will take to get back to the car.

00:49:54.420 --> 00:49:56.070
Still have not figured the headboard out.

00:49:56.070 --> 00:49:58.710
So I was like, I can't do this headboard.

00:49:58.710 --> 00:50:00.570
I think it's a bad idea.

00:50:00.570 --> 00:50:01.920
I'm going to take it down

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:03.300
and y'all can figure it out tomorrow

00:50:03.300 --> 00:50:05.063
because Jessie was going to be back.

00:50:07.200 --> 00:50:09.930
The headboard had been
a point of contention,

00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:12.480
or it was representative of
multiple points of contention

00:50:12.480 --> 00:50:16.380
about the way that Jessie
and I were relating

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:17.310
in this situation.

00:50:17.310 --> 00:50:19.410
And also there's a lot
of emotion and I'm trying

00:50:19.410 --> 00:50:20.250
to deal with this.

00:50:20.250 --> 00:50:23.670
What am I thinking? What am I
feeling about Locke leaving?

00:50:23.670 --> 00:50:28.200
And I set the headboard
down and then I'm like,

00:50:28.200 --> 00:50:29.463
okay, I got to go.

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:33.420
I mean, I got to go, meaning
I got to go and I want us

00:50:33.420 --> 00:50:35.130
to be able to say goodbye.

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:36.180
Yeah.

00:50:36.180 --> 00:50:39.333
And so at that moment
Locke's like, "Oh man.

00:50:41.325 --> 00:50:46.325
This is it."

00:50:46.864 --> 00:50:48.520
This is it.

00:50:48.520 --> 00:50:50.760
So he does this thing
with Shepherd and then

00:50:50.760 --> 00:50:52.950
me and him hug and I say,
I don't know what I said,

00:50:52.950 --> 00:50:54.900
we said some things and I'm tearing up.

00:50:57.960 --> 00:51:00.300
Jessie, in that moment,

00:51:00.300 --> 00:51:03.210
Jessie is like, "Okay, I'm
going to get this trash,"

00:51:03.210 --> 00:51:07.950
or she says something
logistical in that moment.

00:51:07.950 --> 00:51:10.343
Cause she's staying.
Because she's staying.

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:14.757
And I was like, "What are you doing?"

00:51:16.050 --> 00:51:16.883
I was like,

00:51:19.237 --> 00:51:20.547
"I'm leaving!"

00:51:22.410 --> 00:51:24.710
I'm like, me and Shepherd
are not coming back.

00:51:28.620 --> 00:51:31.080
Didn't you hear Locke say, "This is it?"

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:34.080
And so, I'm not trying to
throw Jessie under the bus,

00:51:34.080 --> 00:51:35.673
or the headboard or anything.

00:51:36.660 --> 00:51:39.570
But she was coming back
and it had not quite,

00:51:39.570 --> 00:51:42.210
and she's processing this on her own,

00:51:42.210 --> 00:51:44.220
it had not hit her.

00:51:44.220 --> 00:51:46.770
Cause she's trying to get all
this stuff done in his room

00:51:46.770 --> 00:51:48.660
and that hit her in the moment.

00:51:48.660 --> 00:51:51.930
Oh yeah, this is their moment,
this is the last moment

00:51:51.930 --> 00:51:53.370
that they have together.

00:51:53.370 --> 00:51:54.570
Not ever.

00:51:54.570 --> 00:51:57.660
But as she's leaving.

00:51:57.660 --> 00:51:59.400
And so we are kind of at each other,

00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:01.420
but that immediately

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:06.003
impacted the nature of the process.

00:52:08.100 --> 00:52:10.260
And then of course she
was like, "I'm sorry.

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:11.820
I'm coming back.

00:52:11.820 --> 00:52:13.470
I'm just thinking about
the fact that we're going

00:52:13.470 --> 00:52:15.180
downstairs and we've got all
this stuff we need to take."

00:52:15.180 --> 00:52:17.520
I was like, "Trust me, I'm
the one that would usually

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:18.927
do exactly what you did.

00:52:18.927 --> 00:52:21.540
I'm just surprised that you did it."

00:52:21.540 --> 00:52:24.990
But Locke and I, he went
out into the hall with us

00:52:24.990 --> 00:52:28.470
and I hugged him three or four times.

00:52:28.470 --> 00:52:29.880
There was not one moment.

00:52:29.880 --> 00:52:32.243
It was just like, I guess
I'll just hug you again.

00:52:37.410 --> 00:52:40.330
It sort of perfectly encapsulated just how

00:52:41.640 --> 00:52:44.410
you can't script this
thing out in a way that

00:52:45.630 --> 00:52:48.630
make it perfect and make it
exactly the memorable thing

00:52:48.630 --> 00:52:50.490
where you're going to say
exactly the right thing

00:52:50.490 --> 00:52:51.390
at the right time.

00:52:52.980 --> 00:52:54.900
He's going to remember this forever.

00:52:54.900 --> 00:52:57.730
And he is probably going to
remember that in this moment

00:52:59.430 --> 00:53:01.740
I snapped at Jessie because
she was trying to take

00:53:01.740 --> 00:53:03.750
some trash down the stairs.

00:53:03.750 --> 00:53:05.247
But in a sense it's like-

00:53:05.247 --> 00:53:06.965
No, he won't remember anything.

00:53:06.965 --> 00:53:08.850
That's my parents.

00:53:08.850 --> 00:53:09.683
Maybe.

00:53:09.683 --> 00:53:11.273
I don't think he'll remember that.

00:53:14.520 --> 00:53:16.440
And then of course I
ended up talking to him

00:53:16.440 --> 00:53:17.967
three or four more times before-

00:53:17.967 --> 00:53:19.620
Did you turn around and
take a picture of him

00:53:19.620 --> 00:53:20.453
walking back in?

00:53:20.453 --> 00:53:22.433
That was the thing I
was going to ask about.

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:24.473
No.

00:53:24.473 --> 00:53:26.160
I took a picture of all
four of us right before

00:53:26.160 --> 00:53:26.993
he walked out.
Okay.

00:53:26.993 --> 00:53:28.050
Took a selfie of all four of us.

00:53:28.050 --> 00:53:29.040
That's good. That counts.

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:30.420
And Locke has a look on his face.

00:53:30.420 --> 00:53:32.340
Well, I'll show that picture.

00:53:32.340 --> 00:53:34.190
But Locke has a look on his face like

00:53:35.430 --> 00:53:36.690
this is it.

00:53:36.690 --> 00:53:38.090
That's the look on his face.

00:53:39.750 --> 00:53:41.790
He was feeling it for sure.

00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:43.800
We ended up talking three or
four times before I even got

00:53:43.800 --> 00:53:46.680
on the plane because I'm
trying to logistically

00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:47.910
figure things out.

00:53:47.910 --> 00:53:49.660
But the thing I was thinking about,

00:53:53.056 --> 00:53:54.306
have been thinking about,

00:53:55.620 --> 00:53:56.710
is just the

00:53:59.460 --> 00:54:00.843
aspect of,

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:02.513
oh man,

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:06.030
I've done all I can do.

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:08.253
I've kind of been at
this place for a while.

00:54:09.150 --> 00:54:12.960
I've just kind of observed
the way children are

00:54:12.960 --> 00:54:14.020
and known that

00:54:14.940 --> 00:54:17.460
it's much more about what
you do than what you say.

00:54:17.460 --> 00:54:19.920
They're taking a lot more
cues from the way you act

00:54:19.920 --> 00:54:21.990
and the things you do than
the things you tell them.

00:54:21.990 --> 00:54:23.940
They're usually just kind of

00:54:23.940 --> 00:54:26.160
zoning out when you tell them things.

00:54:26.160 --> 00:54:29.670
But that doesn't stop
you from wanting to say

00:54:29.670 --> 00:54:30.933
exactly the right thing.

00:54:31.950 --> 00:54:34.810
If there are three pieces of wisdom

00:54:35.670 --> 00:54:38.610
that he could take with him
into this freshman year,

00:54:38.610 --> 00:54:39.443
what would they be?

00:54:39.443 --> 00:54:42.360
I want to make sure
that I say those things.

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:44.160
It doesn't stop you from doing that.

00:54:45.630 --> 00:54:47.920
But I think there's just this sense of

00:54:49.680 --> 00:54:50.513
all right,

00:54:51.390 --> 00:54:52.223
yes,

00:54:55.080 --> 00:54:57.030
I still am the one

00:54:57.030 --> 00:55:00.340
that's going to be putting money into his

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:04.203
prepaid credit card thing or whatever.

00:55:05.730 --> 00:55:08.010
There's still a connection.
There's still a big connection.

00:55:08.010 --> 00:55:10.010
I'm still the one paying for this thing.

00:55:11.430 --> 00:55:13.260
And he is a phone call away-

00:55:13.260 --> 00:55:16.650
And he's communicative.

00:55:16.650 --> 00:55:17.790
That's the other thing.

00:55:17.790 --> 00:55:19.920
Yeah, yeah, yeah, for sure.
I think that

00:55:19.920 --> 00:55:21.780
he'll talk to people on the phone.

00:55:21.780 --> 00:55:25.380
He's not averse to FaceTime
and stuff like that.

00:55:25.380 --> 00:55:29.673
Didn't you tell me he'll talk to people?

00:55:30.750 --> 00:55:33.423
Locke calls my dad,

00:55:34.320 --> 00:55:35.703
his father-in-law.

00:55:36.870 --> 00:55:38.520
His father-in-law?

00:55:38.520 --> 00:55:41.103
I mean my father-in-law, Jessie's dad,

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:43.563
my brother,

00:55:44.850 --> 00:55:46.110
Jessie's brother-in-law.

00:55:46.110 --> 00:55:47.430
He's good with a phone.

00:55:47.430 --> 00:55:50.970
He's just like, I'm going
to call an older person

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:52.680
and just talk to them.

00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:54.120
And he's done that for years.

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:56.760
So yeah, he instigates a lot of things.

00:55:56.760 --> 00:55:58.078
That bodes well.

00:55:58.078 --> 00:55:59.760
I'm not worried about it.

00:55:59.760 --> 00:56:00.760
He makes friends

00:56:01.920 --> 00:56:03.390
really easily.

00:56:03.390 --> 00:56:06.360
You're not going to
bother him by calling him.

00:56:06.360 --> 00:56:07.193
No.

00:56:09.540 --> 00:56:10.650
That'll make a huge
difference than texting.

00:56:10.650 --> 00:56:13.770
There's still very much a connection.

00:56:13.770 --> 00:56:16.143
Having already built those type of habits.

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:20.260
From my experience, that goes a long way.

00:56:21.634 --> 00:56:25.470
If I get a text from Lily about nothing,

00:56:25.470 --> 00:56:27.320
it's just like, oh, that feels great.

00:56:28.976 --> 00:56:31.593
It's not just me initiating everything.

00:56:32.790 --> 00:56:33.820
One specific

00:56:34.860 --> 00:56:37.083
thing that felt;

00:56:39.330 --> 00:56:41.373
you kind of feel the powerlessness,

00:56:43.200 --> 00:56:44.260
and I think this is

00:56:45.420 --> 00:56:46.660
due mostly

00:56:47.730 --> 00:56:49.500
just to the fact that
your kid's going to be

00:56:49.500 --> 00:56:51.240
out of your supervision
and off in college,

00:56:51.240 --> 00:56:52.113
but also,

00:56:53.070 --> 00:56:54.513
the person that Locke is,

00:56:57.270 --> 00:56:59.850
his room was always a mess back at home.

00:56:59.850 --> 00:57:01.320
You have to tell him to clean stuff.

00:57:01.320 --> 00:57:02.643
He's like me.

00:57:04.020 --> 00:57:05.320
And Jessie's the same way.

00:57:07.500 --> 00:57:10.770
Our house is remarkably
organized for what the nature of

00:57:10.770 --> 00:57:12.033
our personalities is.

00:57:13.319 --> 00:57:14.819
Cause that's not the tendency.

00:57:18.330 --> 00:57:19.163
Things like,

00:57:19.163 --> 00:57:21.120
"Locke I'm telling you
this thing about where

00:57:21.120 --> 00:57:22.527
I'm putting this,"

00:57:23.790 --> 00:57:26.040
and kind of looking in your
eyes and seeing that this is

00:57:26.040 --> 00:57:28.530
not sinking in.

00:57:28.530 --> 00:57:30.840
You're going to have a
room that you're sharing

00:57:30.840 --> 00:57:32.520
with this other guy,

00:57:32.520 --> 00:57:35.760
and you got to keep it clean and

00:57:35.760 --> 00:57:38.163
I'm giving you your passport.

00:57:40.260 --> 00:57:44.523
You are going to be
responsible for your passport.

00:57:46.022 --> 00:57:46.855
You know what I'm saying?

00:57:46.855 --> 00:57:49.440
The sense of trepidation
that I hand this document

00:57:49.440 --> 00:57:52.920
off to him and where is
this going to end up?

00:57:52.920 --> 00:57:55.800
No one will know.

00:57:55.800 --> 00:57:57.453
Including him, definitely.

00:57:59.820 --> 00:58:01.170
I know Lily's different.

00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:03.870
Lily's more buttoned up

00:58:03.870 --> 00:58:05.490
than Locke when it comes to those things,

00:58:05.490 --> 00:58:06.870
so you probably didn't have this

00:58:06.870 --> 00:58:09.300
is she going to be able
to manage the space

00:58:09.300 --> 00:58:10.470
that she's living in?

00:58:10.470 --> 00:58:13.110
No, she's probably going to
manage it for other people too.

00:58:13.110 --> 00:58:14.880
But Locke-
All five of them

00:58:14.880 --> 00:58:16.650
as a matter of fact in her room.

00:58:16.650 --> 00:58:18.200
Locke is different than this.

00:58:19.350 --> 00:58:20.850
But then there's this sense of

00:58:22.220 --> 00:58:24.000
I didn't know I about any of this stuff.

00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:26.070
I was not organized at all.

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:27.480
You figure it out.

00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:30.120
You probably make mistakes.
Well, you had me, Rhett.

00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:31.200
You had me, man.

00:58:31.200 --> 00:58:33.570
And I also lost my passport at one point.

00:58:33.570 --> 00:58:35.790
I don't recall caring too much.

00:58:35.790 --> 00:58:37.660
I was excited to decorate the room

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:40.680
with the occasional poster.

00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:43.470
We didn't have enough
stuff to make a mess.

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:44.303
You know what I'm saying?

00:58:44.303 --> 00:58:46.830
I didn't have enough things
to really make a mess in.

00:58:46.830 --> 00:58:48.630
I was definitely one that initiated,

00:58:48.630 --> 00:58:50.220
all right, we're spending
the next three hours

00:58:50.220 --> 00:58:52.500
cleaning this place.
Yeah.

00:58:52.500 --> 00:58:55.863
And also, I don't remember
ever changing my sheets.

00:58:57.660 --> 00:58:59.850
Yeah, that's a scary thought.

00:58:59.850 --> 00:59:01.800
Did you change your sheets?

00:59:01.800 --> 00:59:04.290
Well, you just feel like it's not needed.

00:59:04.290 --> 00:59:06.840
Yeah. I wasn't a clean freaking that way.

00:59:06.840 --> 00:59:09.510
But Jessie was like, "Okay,
I'm giving you two sets

00:59:09.510 --> 00:59:10.440
of sheets.

00:59:10.440 --> 00:59:11.647
One that will be on the bed"-

00:59:11.647 --> 00:59:13.290
I brought my laundry home.

00:59:13.290 --> 00:59:14.123
I must have brought my sheets home.

00:59:14.123 --> 00:59:17.130
"The other one while
you're washing your sheets,

00:59:17.130 --> 00:59:19.140
which you should do at
least once a month,"

00:59:19.140 --> 00:59:19.990
is what she said.

00:59:20.850 --> 00:59:21.840
Yeah.

00:59:21.840 --> 00:59:24.060
Preferably every two weeks.

00:59:24.060 --> 00:59:26.880
And then I'm thinking, he's
not going to do that at all.

00:59:26.880 --> 00:59:27.870
He's not going to do that.

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:29.460
He's not going to wash his sheets.

00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:31.170
And it'll be fine.

00:59:31.170 --> 00:59:32.460
It'll be fine.

00:59:32.460 --> 00:59:34.590
Some sort of colony will grow in there.

00:59:34.590 --> 00:59:36.660
He'll probably get some
sort of good probiotics

00:59:36.660 --> 00:59:39.450
that go straight through his feet skin.

00:59:39.450 --> 00:59:41.100
I don't know how it works.

00:59:41.100 --> 00:59:42.400
But there is that sense of

00:59:43.680 --> 00:59:45.600
helplessness of

00:59:45.600 --> 00:59:47.587
ah, he's just-

00:59:47.587 --> 00:59:48.499
Can't do it for him.

00:59:48.499 --> 00:59:49.332
He's just going to figure it out.

00:59:49.332 --> 00:59:51.333
I can't hold this for him anymore.

00:59:52.920 --> 00:59:54.820
And you know what? He'll be just fine.

00:59:55.830 --> 00:59:56.790
Well, I hope so.

00:59:56.790 --> 00:59:57.623
I think he will.

00:59:58.710 --> 01:00:01.865
The communication part is so huge.

01:00:01.865 --> 01:00:04.170
He's not dropping off the edge of

01:00:04.170 --> 01:00:06.510
a planet and trying to escape you.

01:00:06.510 --> 01:00:11.340
He was confident enough to
go clear across the country.

01:00:11.340 --> 01:00:13.500
You know what, to his own credit

01:00:13.500 --> 01:00:16.353
and to you and Jessie's credit too.

01:00:20.846 --> 01:00:22.350
He was ready to make that leap.

01:00:22.350 --> 01:00:23.463
You know he's ready.

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:28.890
That's the big thing for me is
just knowing that he's going

01:00:28.890 --> 01:00:32.310
to tell you what's happening.

01:00:32.310 --> 01:00:34.500
He's very upfront

01:00:34.500 --> 01:00:36.363
and he's also communicative.

01:00:40.290 --> 01:00:42.933
That's so much more important
than everything else.

01:00:44.640 --> 01:00:46.770
I'm speaking from experience here.

01:00:46.770 --> 01:00:50.610
Everything that I made a huge
deal about, I'm glad I did,

01:00:50.610 --> 01:00:52.533
but people told me,

01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:56.520
I'd gotten the advice,

01:00:56.520 --> 01:00:59.100
I spent the whole next day
after my first daughter left

01:00:59.100 --> 01:01:00.480
for college crying.

01:01:00.480 --> 01:01:01.313
And then

01:01:03.210 --> 01:01:05.443
that was kind of it,
I was kind of over it.

01:01:05.443 --> 01:01:07.980
I was like, well, okay,
that's helpful to hear.

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:11.970
It was kind of true even
though I pushed it so hard.

01:01:11.970 --> 01:01:14.433
I want to fully experience this.

01:01:17.220 --> 01:01:19.110
I think about with Lincoln,

01:01:19.110 --> 01:01:21.690
he just yesterday just
started his senior year

01:01:21.690 --> 01:01:24.330
of high school, so we'll be
having this conversation.

01:01:24.330 --> 01:01:26.850
But the conversation that we
have about Lincoln will be

01:01:26.850 --> 01:01:28.530
totally different than the
conversation that I had

01:01:28.530 --> 01:01:29.550
about Lily.

01:01:29.550 --> 01:01:31.620
And also, different than
the conversation you had

01:01:31.620 --> 01:01:32.453
about Locke.

01:01:33.835 --> 01:01:35.970
That middle child syndrome.

01:01:35.970 --> 01:01:37.320
Again, I feel for him.
You wouldn't even know

01:01:37.320 --> 01:01:38.153
If he's in college.

01:01:38.153 --> 01:01:39.513
I might not even be there.

01:01:41.097 --> 01:01:41.930
Is he here?

01:01:41.930 --> 01:01:42.900
I'll definitely be there.

01:01:43.800 --> 01:01:44.633
Yeah,

01:01:46.412 --> 01:01:47.280
it's just a different thing.

01:01:47.280 --> 01:01:49.470
But you've gone through it.

01:01:49.470 --> 01:01:51.600
You've checked this box.

01:01:51.600 --> 01:01:54.933
And that, for you, just
talk about you for a second,

01:01:58.050 --> 01:02:00.150
it's a privilege that
you got to experience it

01:02:00.150 --> 01:02:01.440
under these circumstances.

01:02:01.440 --> 01:02:02.380
That you have this

01:02:04.724 --> 01:02:06.720
intact

01:02:06.720 --> 01:02:10.960
and rewarding relationship with Locke that

01:02:13.530 --> 01:02:15.570
it set the

01:02:15.570 --> 01:02:18.870
groundwork for this
experience for you as a dad.

01:02:18.870 --> 01:02:19.860
That you'll never have again,

01:02:19.860 --> 01:02:21.030
you won't have it with Shepherd.

01:02:21.030 --> 01:02:22.780
That is the most important thing.

01:02:24.030 --> 01:02:25.533
We talked about this before.

01:02:26.370 --> 01:02:30.150
That the goal, especially as
you get into the teenage years,

01:02:30.150 --> 01:02:33.513
and kids tend to get cynical,

01:02:34.860 --> 01:02:36.693
they get harder to communicate with,

01:02:37.590 --> 01:02:39.160
they get more private

01:02:40.590 --> 01:02:42.900
and they may even get mean.

01:02:42.900 --> 01:02:44.910
I think that happens
with a lot of teen kids,

01:02:44.910 --> 01:02:47.110
they just kind of get
mean to their parents.

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:54.870
Let me just say, we've
been through those phases.

01:02:54.870 --> 01:02:57.213
It's not like it's been
all smooth sailing.

01:02:58.364 --> 01:03:02.310
He is a teenager, he's
been through these phases

01:03:02.310 --> 01:03:03.750
of a teenager.

01:03:03.750 --> 01:03:05.280
Oh, he's really connecting
with this parent

01:03:05.280 --> 01:03:07.890
and not liking this parent
and going back and forth.

01:03:07.890 --> 01:03:10.210
You cleared a number of hurdles

01:03:11.130 --> 01:03:13.830
at such a point that

01:03:13.830 --> 01:03:15.300
by the time you're sending
him off to college,

01:03:15.300 --> 01:03:16.800
it's not part of it.

01:03:16.800 --> 01:03:18.750
Well, ultimately what I'm saying though is

01:03:19.680 --> 01:03:21.120
at some point along the way,

01:03:21.120 --> 01:03:23.820
we received very good
advice that we have been

01:03:23.820 --> 01:03:26.340
preaching to one another, me and Jessie,

01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:28.773
of maintain the relationship.

01:03:29.610 --> 01:03:30.813
Above all else.

01:03:33.060 --> 01:03:37.530
The first priority needs to be
maintaining the relationship.

01:03:37.530 --> 01:03:40.440
Not getting him to think a certain way.

01:03:40.440 --> 01:03:43.200
Not getting him to do a certain thing.

01:03:43.200 --> 01:03:45.153
I've tried those things.

01:03:47.280 --> 01:03:50.111
Obviously there's principles
and boundaries within which

01:03:50.111 --> 01:03:53.490
you're accomplishing that
that's not coddling, spoiling

01:03:53.490 --> 01:03:55.050
and just giving in to everything.

01:03:55.050 --> 01:03:56.070
Oh, of course.
There's a difference between

01:03:56.070 --> 01:04:00.600
disagreeing and destroying a relationship.

01:04:00.600 --> 01:04:02.730
Sometimes it gets precarious
and I know you're not

01:04:02.730 --> 01:04:03.750
saying that.
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:04:03.750 --> 01:04:05.520
I'm just saying that-

01:04:05.520 --> 01:04:07.050
You don't worship him.

01:04:07.050 --> 01:04:08.310
No, of course not.

01:04:08.310 --> 01:04:09.310
There are these

01:04:10.500 --> 01:04:13.200
moments in which

01:04:13.200 --> 01:04:15.990
I can choose to say
something or do something

01:04:15.990 --> 01:04:17.170
or implement something

01:04:18.240 --> 01:04:19.240
that will be

01:04:20.610 --> 01:04:23.313
long term damaging to the relationship.

01:04:24.450 --> 01:04:26.280
And this is not some
easy thing to navigate,

01:04:26.280 --> 01:04:28.260
cause you're right, this
doesn't mean you don't

01:04:28.260 --> 01:04:29.790
have standards, you
don't have expectations,

01:04:29.790 --> 01:04:31.950
you don't have house rules or whatever

01:04:31.950 --> 01:04:33.240
and you don't give advice.

01:04:33.240 --> 01:04:34.383
We do all that stuff.

01:04:35.220 --> 01:04:38.220
But not at the expense
of the relationship.

01:04:38.220 --> 01:04:41.823
And I'm not 100% sure that
we would've gotten there.

01:04:42.780 --> 01:04:46.710
This was something that our
therapist started telling us

01:04:46.710 --> 01:04:48.780
and I'm sure we would've
gotten it or realized it

01:04:48.780 --> 01:04:50.220
in a different way, but

01:04:50.220 --> 01:04:52.470
when we began to have some
difficulties as Locke kind of

01:04:52.470 --> 01:04:53.910
got into his teenage years,

01:04:53.910 --> 01:04:56.100
and that was the thing
that was the mantra,

01:04:56.100 --> 01:04:58.503
it was like, maintain the relationship.

01:05:00.090 --> 01:05:03.390
I'm so thankful that that
became the mantra because

01:05:03.390 --> 01:05:08.390
this past year has been the
best stage in our relationship.

01:05:12.300 --> 01:05:14.670
Which is exactly what
you want right before

01:05:14.670 --> 01:05:15.753
you send him off.

01:05:19.140 --> 01:05:20.610
Not counting the
headboard thing, but yeah.

01:05:20.610 --> 01:05:21.480
Yeah.

01:05:21.480 --> 01:05:24.030
And one of the things that
he has said that he said

01:05:24.030 --> 01:05:28.380
more and more over the past year,

01:05:28.380 --> 01:05:30.310
and it has increased in frequency is

01:05:31.320 --> 01:05:33.737
he would say things like
"I'm going to miss you guys."

01:05:37.290 --> 01:05:38.880
The things that you want
to hear as a parent.

01:05:38.880 --> 01:05:39.930
He's like, "You know,

01:05:43.050 --> 01:05:45.810
we haven't always agreed on everything,

01:05:45.810 --> 01:05:47.160
and I haven't always
agreed with the things

01:05:47.160 --> 01:05:48.210
that you've done, but

01:05:49.680 --> 01:05:53.367
I know the motivation
has always been love."

01:05:55.110 --> 01:05:56.580
Okay.

01:05:56.580 --> 01:05:57.970
Yeah. Take and store that one.

01:05:57.970 --> 01:06:01.443
I'm like, that is what you
want to hear as a parent.

01:06:05.635 --> 01:06:08.070
I think I've always thought

01:06:08.070 --> 01:06:11.490
maybe when he's in his
30s and he's got kids,

01:06:11.490 --> 01:06:13.023
he'll come back and he'll say,

01:06:13.927 --> 01:06:15.717
"The motivation was always love."

01:06:19.050 --> 01:06:20.583
But to hear it now was like,

01:06:21.510 --> 01:06:22.343
wow.

01:06:24.630 --> 01:06:25.560
Yeah.

01:06:25.560 --> 01:06:27.300
Don't screw this up, Locke.

01:06:27.300 --> 01:06:28.713
Remember who you are.

01:06:31.440 --> 01:06:33.570
You don't want your dad to
have to come on this podcast

01:06:33.570 --> 01:06:34.970
