01:09:27.180 --> 01:09:28.980
J Dilla passed away

01:09:28.980 --> 01:09:33.980
and he was this super producer of hiphop

01:09:34.260 --> 01:09:35.790
that was,

01:09:35.790 --> 01:09:40.790
in Dan Charnas, the author's perspective

01:09:41.460 --> 01:09:44.970
and argument, he presents
that he created this whole

01:09:44.970 --> 01:09:46.410
new type of time.

01:09:46.410 --> 01:09:48.715
Like musical time,

01:09:48.715 --> 01:09:50.553
which he calls Dilla Time.

01:09:51.480 --> 01:09:52.680
Like a beat signature?

01:09:52.680 --> 01:09:56.610
A beat signature that is
a combination of straight

01:09:56.610 --> 01:09:59.320
and swung time by

01:10:00.360 --> 01:10:02.910
moving tracks independently
from each other

01:10:02.910 --> 01:10:04.500
to create a groove.

01:10:04.500 --> 01:10:07.500
So on one hand, it's like
a lot of music theory,

01:10:07.500 --> 01:10:09.370
and then on the other hand, it's

01:10:11.460 --> 01:10:14.700
a biography of his life and
it kind goes back and forth.

01:10:14.700 --> 01:10:18.000
And there's a lot of hiphop
history that's kind of

01:10:18.000 --> 01:10:20.500
intermingled in both of those two

01:10:21.510 --> 01:10:23.160
tracks that he takes.

01:10:23.160 --> 01:10:26.100
So if you're like me,
you're really into that.

01:10:26.100 --> 01:10:27.450
You'll love "Dilla Time."

01:10:27.450 --> 01:10:30.423
Even though I have not
been a fan of his music,

01:10:32.070 --> 01:10:35.580
I was unknowingly a fan
of many of his tracks

01:10:35.580 --> 01:10:36.630
